    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Since the difference between two consecutive terms is $1$, it implies that the common difference is $1$.\n\nFor an arithmetic sequence, the $n^{text{th}}$ term can be expressed as $a_{n}=a_{1}+(n-1)d$, where $a_{1}$ is the first term and $d$ is the common difference. Substituting this into the given information, we have:\n\\[a_{5} = a_{1} + 4d\\]\n\\[11 = a_{1} + 4(1)\\]\n\\[11 = a_{1} + 4\\]\n\\[a_{1} = 7\\]\n\nSimilarly, for the second term:\n\\[a_{2} = a_{1} + d\\]\n\\[11 = a_{1} + 1\\]\n\\[a_{1} = 10\\]\n\nFinally, the product of the first two terms is:\n\\[a_{1}a_{2} = 7⋅11 = \\boxed{77}\\]",
